  "surprise": "P50-family Gloeckle-style parallel MTP (the pretraining-only t+2 block reads LN(h_t) only, no emb(x_{t+1}); handoff model identical to 136's 4,800,000 params) scored 665/635/611 on seeds 0/1/2 vs 136-fable's 655/673/646: exact means 637.00 vs 658.00, -21.0, paired +10/-38/-35, inside the registered -40..+35 range and below its central 0. Discard. The registered mechanism signal failed: final mtp_loss 0.0595/0.0569/0.0604 vs 136's ~0.055 (predicted 0.07-0.25). Hiding the true t+1 token made the t+2 task harder only early (0.103-0.106 vs 0.081 at 60 s, 0.067-0.071 vs 0.061 at 150 s), so on these proofs x_{t+2} is nearly determined by x_{<=t} and a parallel head adds little lookahead pressure beyond the sequential one. Everything else matched prediction: 3497/3434/3459 steps (136: 3370/3351/3367, +3% from the cheaper (256,256) proj), final main val loss 0.0817/0.0849/0.0801 (136: 0.086/0.083/0.079), held-out greedy 0.9695/0.9685/0.970 (136: 0.965/0.9705/0.968). RL targets solved by round 4 were unchanged, 1139/1110/1103 (136: 1102/1114/1124), so the loss was in dev transfer at >=7, not in RL. Round 1 again failed to predict eval: s0 had 61% round-1 Lean parse failures (29,395 of 48,000; s1/s2 36%/23%) and solved 309 targets in round 1 (s1/s2 614/664), yet scored best at 665. -21 is ~0.6 sd of a difference of 3-seed means, so the firm conclusion is only that the parallel input is not better than DeepSeek's sequential one here.",
